### Validation, queries and endpoint

The graph is validated with SHACL {{ cite("w3c_shacl") }} using pySHACL {{ cite("sw_pyshacl") }},
with SPARQL-based targets and constraints enabled and no inference. There are
{{ f.shacl.shapes }} node shapes for cardinalities, datatypes, identifier formats, evidence tiers,
mapping methods and provenance. Only results of severity Violation stop the pipeline. A test
injects sixteen independent faults into a copy of the graph, covering every node shape, and
requires each to be reported on the right node.

There are {{ f.q.n_sparql | words }} SPARQL queries {{ cite("w3c_sparql") }} over the graph and
{{ f.q.n_sql | words }} SQL queries over the canonical tables in DuckDB {{ cite("raasveldt2019") }}.
Each SQL query must return exactly the rows of its SPARQL counterpart. The graph was also loaded
into an Oxigraph SPARQL endpoint {{ cite("sw_oxigraph") }}, and every query was run through the
SPARQL 1.1 Protocol and compared with the in-process results.

### Reproducibility

The pipeline is a Python {{ f.versions.python }} package with a locked environment; Poppler, a
system binary outside the lockfile, is installed in continuous integration and in the Docker
image, and its version is recorded in the extraction report. Continuous integration on every
pull request runs the linter and the tests, then the pipeline offline from the cached API
responses, and fails if any regenerated output differs from the committed one.

The claim is determinism, not validity: the same PDF, cache, lockfile and Poppler version give
byte-identical outputs. The Turtle file contains no blank nodes; the JSON-LD is written with
sorted keys and sorted arrays (not RDF Dataset Canonicalization); the SHACL report is
canonicalised with RDFLib's graph canonicalisation, which relabels its blank nodes. Continuous
integration runs on one platform (Linux x86-64), and the Docker base image is not pinned by
digest. Resolution results are time-stamped: re-running against the live services gives a newer
snapshot.

Development used one feature branch and one reviewed pull request per stage. Claude Code
(Anthropic) was used as a coding and writing assistant; all curation decisions and results were
reviewed by the author.

## Results

### Extraction and harmonisation

All parts of the article listed in Table 1 were extracted, and the row counts match the numbers
the article states. The second extraction agreed with the first on all
{{ cen.table1.cells_agree }} occupied cells and {{ cen.table1.codes_agree }} codes of Table 1 and on all
{{ (cen.table2.fields_checked + cen.table3.fields_checked) | thousands }} fields of Tables 2 and 3
({{ cen.table3.fields_blank | words }} of them blank). The rendered-image check found ink in each of the
{{ cen.rendered.ink_and_code }} occupied cells and in none of the {{ cen.rendered.no_ink_no_code | thousands }}
empty ones (faintest occupied cell {{ cen.rendered.min_ink_occupied }} dark pixels; darkest empty cell
{{ cen.rendered.max_ink_empty }}). Both checks agreed at their first run; no parser was changed to reach
agreement. Harmonisation produced the canonical tables in Table 2. Every specimen became one
occurrence and every Table 1 code one assertion, except the {{ c.n_folded | words }} new-record codes ("N"),
which qualify a survey code and were merged into it. Counts that cannot be rebuilt from the text
alone are derived in Table S1 (Additional file 1).

