Linking each name to its WoRMS record shows how far the names in the inventory differ from
current taxonomy. WoRMS gives a different accepted name for {{ q03.n }} of the names used in the
article (Table 4):

- **{{ q03.valid | Words }} names the article uses as valid.** For {{ q03.valid_changed | words }} of them the accepted
  name differs, for example *Parazoanthus catenularis*, transferred to *Bergia* by
  {{ citet("montenegro2015") }}. WoRMS lists *Exaiptasia pallida* as an alternative representation
  of *E. diaphana*, pending a ruling on Case 3790; but the Commission has since declined to
  reverse its earlier ruling {{ cite("iczn2017", "iczn2023") }}, so *diaphana* has precedence.
- **{{ q03.invalid | Words }} names the article itself marks with †**, which its legend defines as "species
  currently considered as invalid". One, *Anthopleura cascaia*, is a nomen nudum: as an
  unavailable name it cannot be a synonym, and its accepted name is the WoRMS editors'
  judgement of the intended taxon.
- **{{ q03.label | Words }} old museum labels** that the authors themselves re-identified.
- **{{ q03.spelling | Words }} orthographic variants**: *Bunodosoma cavernata*, which WoRMS holds as an
  unaccepted record of *B. cavernatum*, and three misspellings that matched the accepted record
  directly.

