### What the distribution rests on

The {{ cl.n }} taxon × area cells of Table 1 (printed name × area) were graded by their best
evidence (Fig. 4). {{ cl.specimen }} ({{ cl.pct_specimen }}%) rest on a listed specimen whose identification
the article confirms; {{ cl.code_only }} on a survey or museum code without one; {{ cl.lit_only }}
({{ cl.pct_lit_only }}%) on literature only, of which {{ cl.agudo_alone }} ({{ cl.pct_agudo_alone }}%) cite
one monograph and nothing else {{ cite("agudo1987") }}; a cell citing that monograph and another
source counts as "other". Counted in codes rather than cells, the monograph accounts for
{{ f.codes.agudo }} of the {{ f.codes.literature }} literature codes ({{ f.codes.pct_agudo_of_literature }}%). It is cited
through the article and was not consulted here, so whether its records rest on vouchers, and
what its names denote today, is unknown.

The split by sampling is the informative one. In the {{ cl.sampled_areas }} areas the authors sampled,
{{ cl.sampled.pct_specimen }}% of the cells rest on a confirmed specimen and {{ cl.sampled.pct_lit_only }}% on literature only;
in the other {{ (cl.areas_per_region.values() | sum) - cl.sampled_areas }} areas, {{ cl.not_sampled.pct_specimen }}% and
{{ cl.not_sampled.pct_lit_only }}%. Only in sampled areas could a literature-only cell mean that the taxon was
looked for and not found; elsewhere it was not looked for. Across regions, which hold different
numbers of areas of very different size, the literature-only share ranges from
{{ cl.by_region["Oceanic Islands"].pct_lit_only }}% to {{ cl.by_region["Central Coast"].pct_lit_only }}%. Merging names with the same accepted
name gives {{ cl.accepted_n }} cells and the same literature-only share ({{ cl.accepted_pct_lit_only }}%).

