## Discussion

**Keeping the publication intact.** The central design choice was to treat the article as data
to be preserved, not as text to be corrected. Names are kept as name usages and linked to
current records with SKOS and SSSOM rather than replaced, so the 2016 inventory and today's
taxonomy coexist in one graph, each with its own provenance (Table 4). Provider disagreements
about circumscription, such as NCBI's treatment of *Bunodeopsis antilliensis*, are reported in
Additional file 4 rather than encoded in the graph. Keeping the specimen tables apart from the
distribution matrix is what made the cross-table comparison possible.

**Judgement where it is needed, code everywhere else.** Most of the work is deterministic and
tested: extraction and its checks, rule-based harmonisation, exact matching, validation. The
decisions that need expertise fill {{ c.curated_rows }} rows in {{ c.curated_files | words }} curated files. Each carries
its evidence and the date it was reviewed; a decision that no longer applies is detected,
because the pipeline fails on an unused override; and the mapping justification says whether a
machine or a person asserted a link. It also makes the decisions auditable by someone other
than the curator, which matters here because the curator is a co-author of the source.

**Reading the cross-table differences.** Read per cell, most differences between the matrix and
the specimen tables have a cause in the article itself, or are differences of coding rather than
of presence. Of those that remain, most are survey codes without a voucher, which the legend
does not promise. The {{ x.unexplained_by_kind.museum_code_no_specimen | words }} museum codes without a specimen in Appendix 2 are the
clearest residual, since the legend refers code "a" to that appendix; they may reflect specimens
not re-examined. These are differences between tables, conditional for some of them on curated
locality links, not demonstrated errors, and the list is offered to the source authors.

**Fitness for use.** The graph is suitable as a literature-based checklist with graded evidence:
for tracing each record to its evidence, choosing records by evidence tier, and reconciling the
2016 names with current taxonomy. It is not, as it stands, suitable for species distribution
models or for area-of-occupancy estimates in red-list assessments. No area is georeferenced, the
{{ f.loc.areas }} areas range from a single beach to a gulf and include lagoons and gulfs whose salinity
regime is not recorded, literature records have no date, and depth is not recorded. Presence is
reported without absence or effort, beyond the flag on the six sampled localities. The article
does not state whether any species is introduced, and no establishment means is recorded. Nor is
the graph an expected-species list for environmental DNA: it carries no event-level environment,
and it lists sessile benthic taxa for which eDNA shedding, and the assignment of reads to host
or symbiont, are open questions.

**Publishing the records.** The two classes of occurrence need different routes. The
{{ c.occurrence }} specimens can go to GBIF as occurrences, once a georeference layer exists. The
{{ c.distribution }} Table 1 assertions, undated and located only by area name, should not: they
belong in a checklist with a distribution extension (area, occurrence status, source and
evidence tier), and an assertion backed by a specimen points to it through
`dwc:associatedOccurrences`, so that no organism is counted twice.

**Limitations.** Eight limitations apply.

- **One article.** The parser relies on the layout of this article and refuses unexpected
  layouts. Only the layers after extraction are expected to transfer.
- **Curation by a co-author.** The {{ c.curated_rows }} curated rows were made and reviewed by a
  co-author of the source. An independent audit by an anthozoan taxonomist is required before
  any journal submission.
- **What the extraction checks cover.** The two extractions read the same text layer and share
  layout knowledge; the rendered-image check covers presence in Table 1, not the identity of a
  code or the specimen tables.
- **Habitat per species.** Habitat is attached to species as the article reports it; half of the
  names have none, ENVO lacks a class for coral rubble, and biotic associations are not modelled.
- **No georeferencing.** Areas are kept as named, without datum, salinity regime or depth.
- **Heuristic scores.** The scores of fuzzy matches only ordered candidates for review.
- **Placeholder namespaces.** The namespaces are not yet registered, so the graph's IRIs do not
  dereference, and the repository has no archival DOI yet.
- **A dated snapshot.** Resolution reflects the external services on the access dates.

**Next steps**, in order:

1. An independent audit of the curated decisions, and a review of the residual cross-table
   differences with the source authors.
2. An archival deposit with a DOI, and registered namespaces.
3. A derived georeference layer (point-radius with coordinate uncertainty, datum, Marine Regions
   identifiers), before any publication to GBIF.
4. Publication: specimens as occurrences, Table 1 as a checklist with a distribution extension.
5. Biotic associations as interactions, and crosswalks from the habitat vocabulary to CMECS and
   the IUCN Habitats Classification Scheme; a request for an ENVO class for coral rubble.
6. Molecular and morphological data joined to the same specimens through their stable keys.
7. Periodic re-resolution, reporting changes in Table 4 and in NCBI coverage.

## Conclusions

A species inventory published as a PDF can be made machine-actionable without rewriting it.
In this case:

- every one of the {{ s.t1_codes }} distribution codes and {{ s.t2 + s.t3 }} specimen records is traceable
  to its page, table and row, and two independent checks agree with every one of them;
- every identifier link records how, when and by whom it was made;
- the graph has no SHACL violation and is rebuilt byte for byte from its committed inputs.

The process graded the inventory's evidence. Only {{ cl.pct_specimen }}% of its taxon × area cells rest on a
specimen whose identification the article confirms, and {{ cl.pct_lit_only }}% rest on literature alone,
most of them on one monograph. Its three tables differ in {{ x.cells }} cells; once
re-identifications, coding differences and the survey codes that the legend does not tie to a
voucher are set aside, {{ x.unexplained - x.unexplained_by_kind.survey_code_no_voucher }} remain, some of them conditional on
curated locality links. Reading any one table of the article shows none of this.

